     1	"""attribute — diff violation sets of a baseline and a mutated-spec run, then label with the manifest.
     2	
     3	Both runs must exercise the same SUT build; only the spec differs. Then:
     4	  NEW        fingerprint only in the mutated run
     5	               on a mutated operation  -> label = manifest expected label (injected SPEC defect)
     6	               on an untouched op      -> label = UNEXPLAINED (nondeterminism or cross-op effect)
     7	  VANISHED   fingerprint only in the baseline run -> MASKED: the mutation hid a real
     8	             mismatch (e.g. REQUEST_LOOSE hiding INVALID_ACCEPTED) — reported, not labelled
     9	  PERSISTENT in both runs -> pre-existing mismatch, needs manual labelling (codebook §3)
    10	
    11	Usage: python3 attribute.py BASELINE_violations.jsonl MUTATED_violations.jsonl MANIFEST.json > attributed.csv
    12	Note: tools that use the spec to generate requests (all black-box fuzzers) send
    13	different traffic under a mutated spec; NEW/VANISHED therefore mixes oracle
    14	changes and traffic changes. Replaying the baseline requests against the
    15	mutated spec isolates oracle-side effects (use conformance.py on the baseline
    16	exchanges with the mutated spec).
    17	"""
